agent-threat-rules: dual-use disclosure

This package is declared dual-use under the npm Dual-Use Content Policy
(https://docs.npmjs.com/policies/dual-use/).

What the package is

agent-threat-rules (ATR) is an open detection standard for attacks against AI
agents: prompt injection, tool and skill poisoning, credential and context
exfiltration, privilege escalation through agents, and related threats. It
contains a set of YAML detection rules and a JavaScript engine and CLI that
evaluate those rules against text.

Why it contains attack strings

A rule has to describe an attack in order to detect it, so the files under
rules/ contain attack strings on purpose:

- detection patterns (regular expressions and literal fragments) that match
  prompt-injection text, script and HTML injection payloads, shell commands,
  credential-exfiltration command lines and similar content;
- descriptions and false-positive notes that name the techniques a rule
  covers.

The rules published here leave out each rule's test samples (test_cases and
evasion_tests): the attack inputs a rule must detect and the benign inputs it
must not. Some of those samples are complete attack snippets, and the engine
does not use them. They remain in the source repository, where the project's
own tests run them.

These strings are data. The engine matches the patterns, as regular expressions
or as literal strings, against text supplied by the caller. Rule content is
never executed, evaluated as code, or passed to a shell.

What the package does not do

- It has no preinstall, install or postinstall scripts.
- It makes no network requests by default. Each network path listed below
  stays off until the user turns it on.
- It does not run rule content, and it starts no process through a shell.

Network access (all opt-in)

- Threat Cloud reporting and the Threat Cloud CLI commands contact only a URL
  the user supplies (--tc-url, ATR_TC_URL or TC_URL). There is no built-in
  default for them.
- The LLM-based semantic judges run only when the caller turns them on and
  supplies an API key: in code through createOpenAICompatibleJudge or the
  engine's semanticModule option, and in the CLI through --semantic or
  ATR_SEMANTIC. If no base URL is set (baseUrl, --semantic-base-url,
  ATR_SEMANTIC_BASE_URL or LLM_BASE_URL), they send the text being evaluated
  to https://api.openai.com. Setting a base URL points them at another
  endpoint, including a local model.
- The optional embedding module needs @xenova/transformers, which this package
  does not install. If the user installs it, that library downloads its model,
  from the Hugging Face Hub by default, the first time the module is used.

Local processes

- The atr tc pull command, for operators of a Threat Cloud endpoint, checks
  that each rule the endpoint returns has a well-formed rule id and one of the
  schema's categories before writing it under the rules directory. It then
  runs the package's own rule validator on the file in a separate Node.js
  process.
- The evaluation and benchmark scripts under dist/eval/ (the eval harness CLI
  used by run-eval, run-pint-benchmark, run-hackaprompt-benchmark and
  skill-benchmark) start git and find through the measurement writer, to
  record the commit they measured and count the rule files.
- These processes are started without a shell, and none of them runs rule
  content.

Intended use

Defensive: detecting attacks in AI agent inputs, outputs, tool calls, MCP
traffic and agent skill files, inside agent frameworks, gateways, CI pipelines
and security audits.

